well now it's a question the government  and the Labour Party is still tiptoeing  around does leaving the EU also mean  leaving the single market while the  former shadow home secretary and  Manchester mayoral hopeful Andy Burnham  thinks it should and he joins me now  very good morning to mr. Burnham and get  on to that in just one minute but I just  want your instant reaction to this news  about all grieve now you've been amongst  those campaigning for a public inquiry  into or greed it seems that's not going  to happen but we're going to get some  kind of release of the many documents I  suspect that the police forces the  various police forces involved have held  you welcome that Oh certainly and its  credit to a vet Cooper and the Home  Affairs Select Committee who have  carried on pressing the home secretary  so this is a positive development I  can't see how any government can deny  people the truth about what happened and  as I've repeatedly said Dermot we won't  have the full truth about Hillsborough  until we also know what happened five  years earlier after or grief because in  my view the same police force use the  same tactics it tried to blame the  miners in the same way it would later  try to blame the Liverpool supporters  okay so briefly on that you think the  charge of a public inquiry is still  alive well I think it's got to be  because we need to see what these files  reveal and if they reveal evidence of  wrongdoing both in the police force or  possibly within the government of the  day then that will need to be looked at  further so yes I think this is a  positive development and as I say it's  credit to affect Cooper in the Home  Affairs Select Committee okay thanks for  that now on to the single market isn't  it party policy that the UK tries to  remain within this single market you  think actually leaving it might be good  for the north well I think to understood  them at things are getting very confused  aren't they the government isn't setting  out its plans and so many people out  there are still rerunning the referendum  campaign I think what's got to happen  people have got to go back to what the  public voted for and here in Greater  Manchester people quite clearly voted  for a change to the current freedom of  movement rules that is that is the  starting point it seems to me in this  debate and what I am saying is let's  have a brexit plan that works for  way to Manchester that means it must  respond to what people voted for so I  would argue that does need to be a  system that offers more control but it's  got to be balanced we want to continue  to welcome people from around the world  to work here but that's our starting  point the single market can't be our  starting point because that in the end  here was not what people voted on so  just what do you have in mind because  you know we've been hearing from labor  for an awful long time now the  acceptance of a need for some kind of  controls on immigration could you be  more explicit for us then does it  involve visas permits point system what  the government has got to set out its  intentions but I believe we could have a  system that is more linked to people  coming to fill a specific job in the  labor market rather than speculative  free movement which has actually caused  some quite difficult challenges in parts  of Greater Manchester we've seen  pressure downward pressure on wages now  the public are right to raise concerns  about that Dermot so we need a system  that affords greater control that allows  us to bring people to work here and  contribute to our economy in our society  but also deals with the negative effects  of full free movement which has been  downward pressure on wages but also  pressure on primary schools GP services  we need a system that balances those  things together and that's what I'm  calling for is it because you're  intending to be leaving Parliament I  guess if you win the mayoral tea that  you feel free now to talk out so openly  against party policy against what the  leader says if you look back actually  over a long time I've been making these  arguments I made these arguments in  cabinet when I was picking up concern  about the way free movement was working  back in the the latter part of the last  decade and when I stood for the Labour  Party leadership both times I made  similar arguments you know I'm not  somebody who says yeah we just abandon  our relationship with Europe certainly  not and I hoped that we may be able to  reform free movement from within the EU  but the public have said otherwise  they've asked us to reassess our  relationship with the European Union  particularly on this question of free  movement you know it hasn't been working  for parts of Greater Manchester as I  just said so what we need to do is start  there except the public's call for a  system of more control but balance that  by securing maximum access to the single  market what is your analysis as you as I  say you hope to turn your back on the  Labor Party nationally as someone said  become the Boris of the North I know you  you're not going to accept that but what  do you read into these two by-elections  recently in sleaford and in Richmond  where the Labour Party's been hammered  in a massively remained constituency and  then hammered again in one that was  overwhelmingly leave do you fear that  the party nationally is no longer  relevant no I don't fear that labour has  got a huge role to play going forward  standing up for for working people being  a party that speaks for fairness what  we've got to do though is respond to the  times we're living through we are living  through it feels to me like something of  a political earthquake where there is a  major backlash going on against our  centralized Westminster system of  government Westminster in my view has  failed large parts of the north of  England you know it led our traditional  industry go without giving us any real  help it's focused on selling off council  housing rather than building the new  homes that we need and it's given us a  housing crisis and it's failed to offer  hope to young people now people at the  referendum were calling for change on  all of those things and they were also  saying you haven't been listening to us  about the way free movement is affecting  affecting us so this is a moment of real  change I think Dermot where we need to  embrace devolution to the english  regions and it to be quite proper for me  to argue for what benefits greater  manchester and that might be different  from what what people say at a national  level but it's just because you're a  seasoned campaigner you know what it  comes down to on the doorstep and you  will have heard this from the Labour  canvases in sleaford they say they were  knocking on door steps of people who  previously voted labour and they were  saying are you remain or leave what is  party policy your canvas is had to say  well it's more nuanced than that and  people just went and it seemed didn't  vote for labour and as you know from the  British elections survey didn't you that  more people now align them sell  as a remainder or a lever than they do  with political parties this is a real  problem for labor what we've got to make  sense don't we do with all the times  that we're living through and I don't  think people want us to be in the lib  dem position of trying to reopen the  referendum what labour has got to do is  find a balanced approach to brexit and  as I say a brexit here that works for  greater manchester and I think maybe  Labour's problem has been over the last  15 20 years is we've tried to run a  single line to apply to the whole of the  UK and the problem with that is things  look different here from great dimension  than they do in London and that's why  this change is important now that it's  right that I can articulate how people  here feel and develop a response that is  right for people here and that shouldn't  in any way threaten the if you like that  the National the National Party and so  that's the change I think we need to  move towards and I'm quite clear that we  need to look at brexit and what it means  for greater manchester in a different  way because there's a balance also about  the single market it may well be the  best answer for the north of England to  stay in the single market but we also  need to think about do we need more  flexibility on state aid rules to help  rebuild our industrial base and it's  those trade-offs I think we need to  debate just very briefly if you win the  mayoral t you're going to stand down as  an MP for me and that it said might go  to you keep your seat in that  by-election well that's a that's a lot  of assumptions there there is no there  is no haven't won and there's no vacancy  at the moment but I I do hear that mr.  Nuttall talk is talking of parachuting  himself into to this area well to be  honest I think that's part of what is  wrong with politics people don't like  the idea that people use seats as a  place of convenience for them you know  they want to see people who who come  from the area that they that they  represent but mr. nuts all is also an  advocate of privatization in the  National Health Service i look forward  to him explaining and justifying those  views on the on the streets of lee and  in the middle of town so you are going  to stand down we're right to be  successful next year of course I  wouldn't seek to do  two jobs that would not be right but  that is some way in the future you know  I'm not taking anything for granted what  I am saying is if I want to represent  greater manchester then we need to start  dealing with what people here was saying  at the referendum and that's what I'm  focused on okay well thanks very much  mr. Burnham very good to talk to you  Andy burn thank you
 

